"# TITLE: \"Whispers from the Forgotten Folio\"\n\n## INTRODUCTION (3 minutes):\n\nKEEPER: Welcome to our Call of Cthulhu adventure set in the autumn of 1937, as the world teeters on the brink of another great war. Tonight, we find ourselves in Arkham, Massachusetts, where strange occurrences have been reported at Miskatonic University. Before we begin, let's meet our investigators. Phoenix, would you introduce your character?\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton Blackwood Hargrove is a 42-year-old rare book conservator at the Miskatonic University Library. He comes from old money but has dedicated his life to preserving knowledge rather than social climbing. He's tall, thin, with prematurely silver hair and wire-rimmed spectacles. Thornton is methodical, detail-oriented, and possesses an encyclopedic knowledge of ancient texts. His colleagues find him somewhat aloof, but those who know him well understand his passion for preservation transcends social niceties.\n\nKEEPER: Thank you, Phoenix. Morgan, tell us about your character.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Dr. Philip Haversham Montgomery is a 38-year-old botanical pathologist specializing in rare and exotic plants. He joined Miskatonic's Agricultural Research Division after expeditions in South America and Southeast Asia. Philip has a robust build, tanned skin, and a meticulously trimmed mustache. He's pragmatic and scientifically minded, but has witnessed enough strange phenomena during his travels to keep an open mind. He's been consulting with the library on the preservation of ancient herbals and botanical manuscripts.\n\nKEEPER: Excellent. And Sage, who are you playing tonight?\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Dr. Sebastian Rothschild is a 45-year-old antiquarian book restorer who recently joined Miskatonic University as a visiting scholar. He's renowned for his expertise in medieval manuscripts and ancient languages. Sebastian is of medium height with a slight stoop from years bent over manuscripts. He has a neatly trimmed beard with streaks of gray and always dresses impeccably in three-piece suits. Though brilliant, he's socially awkward and often speaks in tangents about obscure historical facts. He harbors a secret fascination with occult texts that he keeps carefully hidden beneath his scholarly reputation.\n\nKEEPER: Perfect. Our story begins on a dreary October evening as an unusual package arrives at Miskatonic University's Special Collections department. The three of you have been summoned by Dr. Armitage, the head librarian, to examine what appears to be a previously unknown medieval manuscript recently acquired from an anonymous donor. Little do you know that this seemingly routine assessment will draw you into a mystery that spans centuries and threatens to unravel the very fabric of reality itself.\n\n## SCENE 1 (7 minutes):\n\nKEEPER: The library's clock tower chimes eight times as you make your way through the gothic corridors of Miskatonic University's main building. Outside, an unseasonable fog has descended upon Arkham, muffling sounds and casting eerie halos around the streetlamps. You arrive at Dr. Armitage's office, where the elderly librarian greets you with obvious anxiety. \"Thank you all for coming at such short notice,\" he says, gesturing to a large wooden crate on his desk. \"This arrived yesterday with no return address, only instructions that it should be examined by our most qualified experts.\" The crate contains a leather-bound volume approximately 14 inches tall and 10 inches wide, its cover unmarked except for a strange symbol embossed in the center \u2013 a spiral pattern that seems to shift slightly when viewed from different angles. \"I've never seen anything quite like it,\" Armitage admits. \"The parchment appears medieval, but the binding technique suggests something much older. Most concerning are the effects it seems to have on those who handle it for too long. My assistant complained of hearing whispers after cataloging it, and I myself have experienced... unusual dreams since it arrived.\"\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton approaches the book cautiously, adjusting his spectacles. \"Fascinating. May I?\" Without waiting for permission, he pulls a pair of cotton gloves from his pocket and carefully lifts the volume. \"The binding is indeed unusual. Not European, I'd wager. Perhaps Middle Eastern or even further east?\" He examines the spiral symbol. \"Dr. Armitage, have you attempted to translate any of the text inside?\"\n\nKEEPER: Dr. Armitage shakes his head. \"I've only skimmed it briefly. The language appears to shift between Latin, Greek, and something I couldn't identify. That's partly why I called for Dr. Rothschild's expertise.\" As Thornton handles the book, make a POW roll to see if you notice anything unusual.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled a 27, under my POW of 65. Success.\n\nKEEPER: As you hold the book, Thornton, you feel a subtle vibration through your gloves, almost like a heartbeat. More disturbing is the momentary sensation that the spiral on the cover is slowly rotating clockwise, though when you blink and look again, it appears static. You also notice that while the book appears old, it lacks the expected musty odor of ancient texts. Instead, there's a faint scent reminiscent of sea salt and something metallic.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian steps forward eagerly, his eyes widening behind his spectacles. \"If I may, Mr. Hargrove?\" He extends his gloved hands. \"A shifting language suggests a palimpsest or perhaps a more... esoteric textual phenomenon. I've encountered similar characteristics in certain banned texts from the Byzantine era.\"\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton reluctantly hands the book to Sebastian. \"By all means, Doctor. But I recommend caution. There's something... peculiar about this volume.\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian carefully opens the book, examining the first few pages. \"Extraordinary! The script appears to be a variant of medieval Latin, but interspersed with symbols I've only seen in certain hermetic texts from Alexandria.\" He flips through more pages, becoming increasingly animated. \"And here\u2014this section transitions to what appears to be pre-Sumerian pictographs! This defies all conventional understanding of textual chronology.\" I'd like to make a Library Use roll to see if I recognize any specific references or passages.\n\nKEEPER: Go ahead.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 14, well under my Library Use of 82. Critical success!\n\nKEEPER: Sebastian, as you carefully examine the text, your extensive knowledge allows you to recognize several key elements that others would miss. The book appears to be a compilation of various esoteric knowledge, spanning multiple cultures and time periods. Most significantly, you identify a recurring reference to something called \"The Whispering Tide\" and \"Those Who Dwell Beyond the Veil.\" More concerning is a passage you translate that reads: \"When the stars align and the tide recedes, the door stands ajar, and whispers become voices.\" You also notice that certain pages contain botanical illustrations of plants you've never seen before\u2014plants with anatomical features that seem impossible, combining elements of fungi, algae, and flowering species in ways that defy natural taxonomy.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Dr. Montgomery, who has been quietly observing, suddenly perks up at the mention of unusual plants. \"May I see those illustrations, Sebastian?\" He peers over Dr. Rothschild's shoulder, his scientific curiosity piqued. \"These are... well, they're biologically impossible. This one appears to have gill-like structures, but on what seems to be a flowering plant. And this one...\" He points to another illustration. \"The root system resembles cephalopod tentacles more than typical plant structures.\" I'd like to make a Biology roll to see if I can make any sense of these botanical anomalies.\n\nKEEPER: Roll for it.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): I rolled a 43, under my Biology skill of 75. Success.\n\nKEEPER: Philip, your botanical expertise tells you that while these plants couldn't possibly exist according to known biological principles, the illustrations are rendered with scientific precision. Whoever drew these wasn't imagining fantasy plants but documenting specimens with the eye of a trained naturalist. Most disturbing is your recognition that one illustration bears a striking resemblance to specimens you've heard rumors about\u2014plants allegedly discovered during the ill-fated Pabodie Expedition to Antarctica several years ago, samples of which were supposedly destroyed after causing unexplained laboratory incidents.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip's face pales slightly. \"Gentlemen, these illustrations... they bear uncomfortable similarities to specimens I've heard described from the Antarctic expedition. Specimens that reportedly caused several researchers to suffer acute psychotic episodes.\" He turns to Dr. Armitage. \"You mentioned your assistant hearing whispers. Where is this person now? Are they experiencing any other symptoms?\"\n\nKEEPER: Dr. Armitage looks troubled. \"Miss Gardner called in sick today. Said she was suffering from severe migraines and... auditory disturbances. I sent Professor Rice to check on her this afternoon, but he hasn't reported back yet.\" As he speaks, there's a knock at the door, and a campus security guard enters, looking concerned. \"Excuse me, Dr. Armitage, but there's been an incident at the botanical gardens. Professor Walters was found unconscious among the specimens, muttering about 'the tide coming in.' The medical staff can't wake him. Dean Halsey thought you should know, given the other... unusual occurrences this week.\"\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton frowns deeply. \"Other occurrences? What exactly has been happening on campus, Dr. Armitage?\"\n\nKEEPER: Armitage sighs heavily. \"Three students reported hearing whispers coming from the walls in the dormitories. A mathematics professor was found writing strange symbols repeatedly on his chalkboard in what appeared to be a trance state. And the groundskeeper claims the fog outside hasn't behaved naturally\u2014says it moves against the wind and sometimes forms patterns.\" The security guard shifts uncomfortably at this. \"There's something else, sir. When they found Professor Walters, he had drawn a symbol on the greenhouse floor.\" The guard sketches on a notepad and shows you all\u2014it's the same spiral pattern that adorns the book's cover.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian exchanges glances with his colleagues. \"I believe our examination of this text should continue in a more... controlled environment. Perhaps the sealed rare manuscripts room?\" He turns to Dr. Montgomery. \"And I think we should visit the botanical gardens immediately afterward. There may be a connection between these plants and the manuscript that requires our combined expertise.\"\n\n## SCENE 2 (7 minutes):\n\nKEEPER: The rare manuscripts room is a windowless chamber in the basement of the library, specially designed to control temperature and humidity. Steel shelves line the walls, holding the university's most valuable and dangerous texts, many of them locked in glass cases. You've set up the mysterious book on a large examination table in the center, surrounded by reference materials. It's been two hours since you began your detailed study, and the clock on the wall shows it's nearly midnight. Outside this sealed room, the fog has grown thicker, pressing against the few windows in the library's upper floors like a living thing seeking entry. As you work, the electric lights occasionally flicker, casting momentary shadows that seem to move independently of your actions. Sebastian has managed to translate more of the text, while Philip has been comparing the botanical illustrations to known specimens from the university's archives. Thornton, you've been examining the physical properties of the book itself. Make a Spot Hidden roll.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled a 32, under my Spot Hidden of 60. Success.\n\nKEEPER: Thornton, as you carefully examine the binding of the book, you notice something unusual about the leather cover. There are tiny, almost microscopic patterns in the material that don't match any animal hide you're familiar with. More significantly, you discover that what you initially took to be decorative stitching along the spine actually forms a tiny line of text in an alphabet you don't recognize. And most disturbing of all, when you run your finger along this stitching, you feel it pulse slightly, as though responding to your touch.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton jerks his hand back from the book. \"Good Lord!\" He composes himself quickly, embarrassed by his outburst. \"Dr. Rothschild, Dr. Montgomery, I believe this book may be bound in... something other than conventional materials. And there appears to be text embedded in the spine itself. Sebastian, might you recognize this script?\" He points to the tiny stitching with a pencil, careful not to touch it again.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian leans in, squinting at the minute text. \"Fascinating... this appears to be a variant of pre-Phoenician script, similar to inscriptions found at certain Mediterranean archaeological sites dating to approximately 2000 BCE.\" He pulls out a small magnifying glass from his waistcoat pocket. \"But there are elements here I've never encountered before. The phonetic structure suggests...\" He trails off, his face growing pale. \"Gentlemen, if my translation is correct, this reads 'Bound in the flesh of those who hear the tide.' Most concerning.\" I'd like to make an Occult roll to see if this connects to anything in my knowledge of esoteric practices.\n\nKEEPER: Roll for it.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 39, under my Occult of 70. Success.\n\nKEEPER: Sebastian, your extensive knowledge of occult practices brings to mind several disturbing possibilities. The phrase \"those who hear the tide\" connects to ancient cults that worshipped entities said to exist in the space between dimensions. These cults believed that certain individuals could hear the \"cosmic tide\" that signaled when the barriers between worlds grew thin. Such individuals were often sacrificed, their remains used in rituals meant to facilitate communication with these entities. More troubling is your recollection of references to a text called \"The Whispering Codex\" in certain forbidden manuscripts\u2014a book said to be bound in the skin of seers and containing knowledge that \"flows like the tide between worlds.\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian removes his glasses, pinching the bridge of his nose. \"I believe we may be dealing with what some occult traditions call 'The Whispering Codex.' It's mentioned in several banned texts as a conduit between our world and... elsewhere. The book itself is said to be semi-sentient, capable of influencing susceptible minds.\" He looks gravely at his colleagues. \"If this is indeed that artifact, we must exercise extreme caution. Its influence may explain the incidents on campus.\"\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip has been comparing the botanical illustrations to reference books spread across the table. \"These plants... they don't exist in nature, yet these drawings show detailed anatomical structures that follow a coherent biological logic\u2014albeit one that contradicts everything we know about plant taxonomy.\" He points to one particularly disturbing illustration. \"This species appears to have both photosynthetic capabilities and structures resembling primitive nervous systems. And this notation here...\" He indicates some text beside the drawing. \"Sebastian, can you translate this?\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian studies the text. \"It reads something like 'Harvested from the shores where the veil thins' and then references a tidal cycle that corresponds to no known lunar pattern.\"\n\nKEEPER: As Sebastian speaks those words aloud, the lights in the room flicker more intensely, and for a moment, you all hear what sounds like a distant rushing of water, though you're in a sealed room in the basement of the library. The sound fades, but leaves behind a faint, rhythmic whispering that seems to come from the book itself. Suddenly, there's an urgent knocking at the door. When you open it, you find Professor Rice, Dr. Armitage's colleague who was sent to check on Miss Gardner. He looks disheveled and deeply disturbed. \"You need to come quickly,\" he says, his voice shaking. \"It's Miss Gardner\u2014and the botanical gardens. Something is... growing.\" Make a SAN roll, all of you, as Rice's appearance and the whispering affect your composure.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled a 72, over my SAN of 60. Failure.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): I rolled a 34, under my SAN of 65. Success.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 83, over my SAN of 55. Failure.\n\nKEEPER: Thornton and Sebastian, you both lose 1D4 SAN points from the combination of the whispering, the implications of what you've discovered about the book, and Professor Rice's disturbing appearance and message. Philip, your scientific mind helps you maintain your composure. Please roll your 1D4 losses.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled a 3, so I lose 3 SAN points.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 2, so I lose 2 SAN points.\n\nKEEPER: As you follow Professor Rice through the now-deserted library and across the foggy campus towards the botanical gardens, he explains what he found. \"Miss Gardner wasn't at her apartment. Her landlady said she left suddenly, talking about 'going to tend the garden.' I found her notes...\" He hands you several pages covered in the same spiral symbol from the book, interspersed with drawings of impossible plants. \"When I went to the botanical gardens to look for her, I found Professor Walters still sedated in the university infirmary, but the greenhouse... something's happening there. Plants growing at impossible rates, specimens that shouldn't exist. And Miss Gardner was there, talking to them, tending them like they were her children.\"\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip quickens his pace. \"Were any of the plants similar to these illustrations?\" He shows Rice the drawings from the book. \"And more importantly, did you notice any spores or pollen in the air? Any unusual scents or mists?\"\n\nKEEPER: Professor Rice nods grimly. \"Yes, exactly like those drawings. And there was a mist inside the greenhouse, though it was different from this fog\u2014greener, somehow. It seemed to be emanating from the plants themselves. Miss Gardner was breathing it deeply, like it was sustaining her. Her eyes...\" He shudders. \"They were different. Darker, with a strange iridescence.\"\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton clutches his notebook tightly. \"Dr. Montgomery, given your expertise, what are we potentially facing here? Could these plants be toxic? Hallucinogenic?\"\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip's expression is grave. \"Potentially both, and worse. If these plants truly resemble the Antarctic specimens I've heard about, they may possess properties that science cannot yet explain\u2014properties that affect the mind directly.\" He turns to Rice. \"Was anyone else exposed to this mist?\"\n\nKEEPER: \"Two graduate students who were working late,\" Rice replies. \"They're both in the infirmary now, unresponsive but physically stable. The dean has quarantined the building, but...\" He trails off as you approach the botanical gardens. The large Victorian greenhouse looms ahead, its glass panels obscured by the unnatural fog, but you can see strange, pulsing lights from within\u2014bioluminescence in colors that plants shouldn't produce. The air grows heavier, more humid as you approach, and that same faint whispering you heard from the book seems to be emanating from the building itself.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian hesitates at the entrance. \"The book, the plants, the whispering\u2014they're all connected to this 'Whispering Tide' phenomenon. I believe we're witnessing some kind of... incursion from another realm of existence.\" He turns to his colleagues. \"We should proceed with extreme caution. Whatever is happening here may be merely the first manifestation of something far larger.\"\n\n## SCENE 3 (7 minutes):\n\nKEEPER: The interior of the botanical gardens is almost unrecognizable. What was once a meticulously organized collection of specimens has transformed into a jungle of impossible flora. Plants with translucent, pulsating bulbs hang from the ceiling, their tendrils swaying despite the still air. Fungi with spiral patterns matching the book's cover grow in concentric circles on the floor, emitting a phosphorescent blue glow. And dominating the central atrium is a massive plant unlike anything you've ever seen\u2014part tree, part coral, part fungus, its trunk twisting in anatomically impossible ways, with what appear to be primitive sensory organs embedded in its bark. The air is thick with a greenish mist that smells of salt water and something unidentifiably alien. And there, tending to this abomination, is Miss Gardner. She moves with jerky, unnatural motions, her skin bearing a slight greenish tint, her eyes entirely black except for tiny pinpricks of light that seem to swirl like distant stars. She turns as you enter, her expression blank. \"You've brought the Codex,\" she says in a voice that sounds like multiple people speaking in unison. \"The garden has been preparing. The tide is rising.\"\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip instinctively covers his mouth and nose with his handkerchief. \"Everyone, don't breathe the mist if you can help it.\" He addresses Miss Gardner cautiously. \"Miss Gardner, can you hear me? It's Dr. Montgomery from the Agricultural Department. You're under the influence of something toxic. We need to get you out of here and to medical attention.\"\n\nKEEPER: Miss Gardner tilts her head at an uncomfortable angle, studying Philip with those disturbing eyes. \"Dr. Montgomery. You study life, yet understand so little of it. These are not toxins but messengers, preparing this place for Those Who Dwell Beyond.\" She gestures to the massive central plant. \"The anchor grows strong. When the Tide fully rises, they will come through.\" As she speaks, you notice movement among the plants\u2014not just the unnatural swaying of tendrils, but what appear to be partially formed humanoid figures composed of plant matter, watching from the shadows. Make a SAN roll, all of you.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled a 52, under my SAN of 57 now. Success.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): I rolled a 78, over my SAN of 65. Failure.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 49, under my SAN of 53 now. Success.\n\nKEEPER: Philip, you lose 1D6 SAN as your scientific mind struggles to rationalize what you're seeing\u2014plant matter taking humanoid form contradicts everything you understand about biology.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): I rolled a 4, so I lose 4 SAN points, bringing me to 61.\n\nKEEPER: As you all take in the horrifying scene, Professor Rice suddenly gasps and points to the central plant-thing. \"The trunk\u2014it's changing!\" Indeed, the twisted trunk is slowly forming what appears to be a face\u2014not human, but not entirely plant either, with features that shift and flow like liquid. More disturbing is the realization that the face resembles the illustrations of certain entities in the Whispering Codex. The air pressure in the greenhouse suddenly drops, causing your ears to pop, and the whispering grows louder, now clearly emanating from both the plants and the mist itself.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian's academic demeanor cracks slightly as he backs toward the door. \"This is a manifestation point\u2014a place where the barrier between dimensions is being deliberately weakened.\" He turns to Thornton. \"The book\u2014it's not just a text, it's a key. Miss Gardner and these plants are preparing for something to come through from the other side.\" He frantically searches his memory. \"I need to make an Occult roll to remember if there's any way to reverse this process.\"\n\nKEEPER: Go ahead.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 27, well under my Occult of 70. Success.\n\nKEEPER: Sebastian, you recall fragments from various forbidden texts that describe similar incursions. The common element in stopping such events involves disrupting the \"anchor\" that connects the dimensions. In this case, that would be both the central plant entity and the Whispering Codex itself. Traditional methods include fire, salt, certain banishing rituals, or\u2014most effectively but dangerously\u2014using the knowledge contained within the summoning object itself against the incursion. The texts warn, however, that those who attempt the latter risk exposure to the same influence that created the problem.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton steps forward, his scholarly reserve giving way to determination. \"We need to destroy both the plant and the book. Dr. Montgomery, would the chemicals in your laboratory be sufficient to kill this... botanical abomination? And Sebastian, if we were to burn the Codex, would that sever the connection?\"\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip, still shaken by what he's witnessing, nods slowly. \"My lab contains several powerful herbicides and fungicides. But this...\" he gestures at the central plant-thing, \"this isn't natural flora. I'm not certain conventional chemicals would affect it.\" He thinks for a moment. \"However, I have been experimenting with compounds derived from certain South American plants known for their ability to kill parasitic fungi. Combined with industrial-strength acid...\" He turns to the others. \"It might work, but we'd need to apply it directly to the root system.\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): \"Burning the Codex might work, but there's a risk\u2014destructive acts against such objects sometimes accelerate the very process they're meant to stop.\" Sebastian's eyes suddenly widen. \"Wait\u2014the text itself mentioned a cyclical nature to this 'Whispering Tide.' If we can determine where in the cycle we are, we might be able to use the book's own knowledge to close the doorway it's opening.\"\n\nKEEPER: As you discuss your options, Miss Gardner suddenly stiffens, her head snapping up to look at the glass ceiling of the greenhouse. \"The alignment approaches. The tide rises.\" Outside, the unnatural fog has begun to swirl in the same spiral pattern found on the book's cover, and through gaps in the mist, you can see that the stars themselves seem to be shifting position, forming unfamiliar constellations. The plant-humanoids begin to move toward you with increasing purpose, while the face in the central trunk opens what approximates a mouth, emitting a sound that is part whisper, part scream\u2014a sound that seems to come from somewhere impossibly distant.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): \"We're out of time for debate.\" Thornton makes a decision. \"Dr. Montgomery, get what you need from your laboratory. Dr. Rothschild and I will use the Codex to determine where in the cycle we are and how to reverse it. Professor Rice, can you keep Miss Gardner occupied? Don't let her interfere with the central plant.\" I want to make a Library Use roll to quickly find relevant information in the Codex about the cycle.\n\nKEEPER: Roll for it.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled a 25, under my Library Use of 75. Success.\n\nKEEPER: Thornton, as you rapidly flip through the Codex, you find a section that details the \"Tidal Cycle of the Veil.\" According to the text, the incursion occurs in five phases: Whispers, Growth, Manifestation, Emergence, and Submersion\u2014when our world is entirely subsumed by the other realm. Based on what you're witnessing, you're currently in the late stages of Manifestation, with Emergence imminent. The text indicates that the cycle can be reversed at any point before full Emergence by performing a ritual that essentially \"turns the tide.\" This requires three elements: a recitation from the Codex itself, a \"vessel of containment\" (which you interpret to mean something that can hold or absorb the energies being released), and the destruction of the physical anchor.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): \"I'll get my compounds. Five minutes\u2014no more!\" Philip dashes from the greenhouse toward his laboratory in the adjacent building. I'd like to make a Chemistry roll to quickly formulate an effective compound when I reach my lab.\n\nKEEPER: Roll for it.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): I rolled a 19, well under my Chemistry of 80. Success!\n\nKEEPER: Philip, you race through your laboratory, grabbing components with practiced efficiency. You combine your experimental fungicide with concentrated acid and several other chemicals, creating a virulent compound that should destroy any organic matter, no matter how alien. You store the mixture in a specialized sprayer designed for applying chemicals to tall trees. As you finish and turn to leave, you notice something disturbing\u2014small spiral growths have begun to appear on the laboratory floor, and the same whispering from the greenhouse is now faintly audible here as well. The incursion is spreading beyond the botanical gardens.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): While Thornton searches the Codex and Philip gets his chemicals, Sebastian examines the greenhouse for anything that could serve as a \"vessel of containment.\" \"Professor Rice, help me look for something that could absorb or contain energy\u2014a container, a specialized piece of equipment, anything!\" I want to make a Spot Hidden roll to find something suitable.\n\nKEEPER: Roll for it.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 67, over my Spot Hidden of 55. Failure.\n\nKEEPER: Sebastian, your search becomes increasingly frantic as you find nothing suitable. However, Professor Rice calls your attention to a sealed glass container in a locked cabinet\u2014a specialized vacuum chamber used for studying plant specimens in altered atmospheric conditions. \"Would this work?\" he asks, breaking the cabinet open with a nearby stool. As he does so, one of the plant-humanoids lunges at him from behind. Make a Dodge roll for Rice!\n\nKEEPER: I'll roll for Rice... and it's a failure. The plant-humanoid's tendril wraps around Rice's throat, pulling him backward. He struggles, dropping the vacuum chamber, which thankfully doesn't break. \"Get it!\" he gasps, fighting against the increasingly tight grip of the plant creature. Meanwhile, Miss Gardner has begun a strange, swaying dance around the central plant, chanting in a language none of you recognize, and the face in the trunk is becoming more defined, its features almost familiar now\u2014reminiscent of classical depictions of certain ancient deities associated with the sea and stars.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton grabs the vacuum chamber while continuing to hold the Codex. \"Sebastian, I've found the ritual! We need to recite this passage while Philip destroys the central plant. This chamber will serve as our vessel.\" He points to a passage in the book written in a script that seems to shift and move on the page. \"Can you read this? My linguistic skills aren't adequate for this dialect.\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian glances at the passage, his eyes widening. \"It's a variant of pre-Sumerian, mixed with something far older. I can pronounce it, but...\" He hesitates. \"Reading such texts aloud comes with significant risk to the reader.\" He straightens his posture, resolute. \"But there's no alternative. I'll begin the recitation when Philip returns.\" I'd like to make a Language (Ancient) roll to properly pronounce the text.\n\nKEEPER: Roll for it.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 42, under my Language (Ancient) of 65. Success.\n\nKEEPER: Sebastian, you find that as you begin to mentally rehearse the words, they seem to resonate in your mind, as if they were always meant to be spoken by you. It's both disturbing and oddly satisfying. You're confident you can recite the passage correctly when the time comes. Just then, Philip bursts back into the greenhouse, carrying his chemical sprayer.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip looks alarmed at the scene before him\u2014Rice struggling with a plant creature, Miss Gardner in her trance-like state, and the central plant-thing now sporting an almost fully-formed face. \"The growth is spreading across campus!\" he shouts. \"Whatever we're going to do, it needs to happen now!\" He readies his sprayer. \"I need to get to the base of that central growth. Can you clear a path?\"\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton looks around desperately for a weapon, spotting gardening tools against the wall. He grabs a pruning saw. \"I'll help Rice and create a distraction. Sebastian, begin the recitation when I give the signal. Philip, be ready to douse that monstrosity the moment Sebastian reaches the critical passage.\" He moves toward Rice, slashing at the plant tendril with the saw. \"Let him go, you vegetative abomination!\" I'd like to make a Fighting roll.\n\nKEEPER: Roll for it.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled an 83, over my Fighting of 40. Failure.\n\nKEEPER: Thornton, you swing the pruning saw but miss the tendril, instead striking a nearby plant that releases a cloud of spores directly into your face. You stagger back, momentarily disoriented, your vision swimming with strange colors and patterns. Make a CON roll to resist the spores' effects.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled a 27, under my CON of 55. Success.\n\nKEEPER: You manage to shake off the worst effects of the spores, though your vision remains slightly distorted. Meanwhile, Philip sees an opening and dashes toward the central plant, dodging grasping tendrils and shifting roots.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip sprints forward, aiming for the base of the central plant-thing. \"Sebastian, start reading! Thornton, get that chamber ready!\" I want to make a Dodge roll to avoid the reaching tendrils.\n\nKEEPER: Roll for it.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): I rolled a 24, under my Dodge of 50. Success.\n\nKEEPER: Philip, you weave through the grasping plant matter with surprising agility, reaching the base of the central growth. Up close, you can see that what appeared to be roots are actually more like tentacles, pulsating with an internal bioluminescence and moving with disturbing purpose. You begin spraying your chemical compound onto these appendages, which recoil and writhe upon contact, emitting a high-pitched keening sound that hurts your ears. The face in the trunk contorts in what appears to be pain or rage.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Seeing Philip begin his attack, Sebastian starts reciting the ancient text, his voice growing stronger with each syllable. The words feel strange in his mouth, as if they're being pulled from somewhere deep within him rather than simply read. As he continues, the vacuum chamber in Thornton's hands begins to glow with an eerie blue-green light, and the air in the greenhouse grows even more charged with energy.\n\nKEEPER: As Sebastian recites the alien words, several things happen simultaneously. The mist in the greenhouse begins to swirl violently, converging toward the vacuum chamber, which glows brighter with each passing second. The plant-humanoids freeze in place, then begin to wither and collapse. Miss Gardner screams\u2014a sound of both rage and despair\u2014and lunges toward Sebastian, her movements inhumanly fast. \"You must not interrupt the tide!\" she shrieks, her voice no longer sounding even remotely human. Make a Dodge roll, Sebastian!\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 72, over my Dodge of 40. Failure.\n\nKEEPER: Miss Gardner slams into Sebastian with surprising strength, knocking him to the ground and causing him to drop the Codex. The book falls open, its pages flipping wildly as if caught in a strong wind, stopping on an illustration that depicts the very scene unfolding in the greenhouse. Sebastian, make a SAN roll as you glimpse this prophetic image and realize the book has been orchestrating these events all along.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 91, well over my SAN of 53. Failure.\n\nKEEPER: Sebastian, you lose 1D6 SAN as you comprehend that you've all been manipulated by the Codex from the beginning.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 3, so I lose 3 SAN, bringing me to 50.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton rushes to Sebastian's aid, still holding the glowing vacuum chamber. \"Philip, how much longer?\" He tries to pull Miss Gardner off Sebastian while maintaining his grip on the chamber. \"Sebastian, you must continue the recitation!\"\n\nKEEPER: Miss Gardner fights with unnatural strength, her fingers elongating into thorn-like protrusions that she attempts to drive into Sebastian's throat. Meanwhile, Philip's chemical compound is having a visible effect on the central plant-thing, which is beginning to collapse in on itself, the face in its trunk screaming silently. Outside, the spiral pattern in the fog is rotating faster, and through the glass ceiling, you can see what appears to be a tear forming in the very fabric of the night sky\u2014a glimpse of something vast and incomprehensible beyond.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip continues dousing the writhing root-tentacles, working his way around the base of the plant. \"Almost there! Whatever that chant is doing, it's working\u2014the plant structure is breaking down at the cellular level!\" He notices the tear forming in the sky. \"Hurry, Sebastian! The barrier is failing!\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Despite Miss Gardner's attack, Sebastian continues the recitation, his voice growing stronger as he nears the end of the passage. Blood trickles from his nose and ears as the words take their toll on his mortal form, but he presses on, determined to complete the ritual.\n\nKEEPER: As Sebastian speaks the final words of the ritual, the vacuum chamber in Thornton's hands pulses with blinding light, drawing in the swirling mist and, seemingly, the very essence of the incursion itself. Miss Gardner screams one final time before collapsing, unconscious but human once more. The central plant implodes with a sound like rushing water, leaving behind nothing but ash and a faint, lingering scent of the sea. The tear in the sky above seals itself, stars returning to their proper positions. Make a final SAN roll, all of you, as the ritual completes and you feel the momentary touch of something ancient and vast withdrawing from our reality.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): I rolled a 43, under my SAN of 57. Success.\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): I rolled a 29, under my SAN of 61. Success.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 81, over my SAN of 50. Failure.\n\nKEEPER: Sebastian, you lose 1D4 SAN as your mind briefly connects with the entity retreating beyond the veil.\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): I rolled a 2, so I lose 2 SAN, bringing me to 48.\n\nKEEPER: The greenhouse falls silent, the unnatural plants withering and crumbling to dust. The vacuum chamber in Thornton's hands grows cold, its glow fading to a faint, pulsing light that seems somehow contained within the glass. The Whispering Codex lies open on the floor, its pages now blank except for the spiral symbol on the cover\u2014a symbol that no longer seems to shift when viewed from different angles. The tide has receded, at least for now.\n\n## CONCLUSION (3 minutes):\n\nKEEPER: Three days have passed since the events at the botanical gardens. The university administration has attributed the incident to a gas leak combined with contaminated specimens, a story readily accepted by those who didn't witness the truth. Miss Gardner and the affected students have recovered, though none remember what happened to them. Professor Walters remains in a sanitarium, his mind too fragmented to be restored. The three of you have been meeting daily in Dr. Armitage's office, documenting your experiences and deciding what to do with the two artifacts in your possession: the now-blank Whispering Codex and the vacuum chamber, which continues to emit a faint glow and the occasional whispered sound when darkness falls.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton carefully wraps the Codex in protective cloth, his hands still trembling slightly when he touches it. \"The book may be dormant now, but I don't believe it's harmless. I've prepared a sealed lead box in the library's most secure vault. We should store it there, with warnings for future scholars.\" He glances at the vacuum chamber. \"As for that... I'm not certain it can be safely contained anywhere on campus.\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian looks haggard, dark circles under his eyes suggesting he hasn't slept well since the incident. \"The chamber contains a fragment of whatever was attempting to come through\u2014a piece of something that doesn't belong in our reality.\" He removes his glasses, rubbing his eyes. \"I've been researching potential containment solutions. There's a colleague at Miskatonic's Antarctic research station who specializes in exotic matter. Perhaps at those temperatures, in that isolation...\"\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip shakes his head firmly. \"After what we witnessed, I don't think distance alone will suffice. Whatever was coming through\u2014whatever is partially trapped in that chamber\u2014it operates beyond our conventional understanding of space.\" He looks gravely at his colleagues. \"I've destroyed all samples from the greenhouse, but I can't shake the feeling that spores might have escaped. And the fog...\" He walks to the window, looking out at the clear autumn day. \"Has anyone else noticed how the weather has been unusually calm since that night? As if nature itself is holding its breath.\"\n\nKEEPER: Dr. Armitage enters the office, carrying a telegram. \"This just arrived from our colleagues at the University of Buenos Aires,\" he says, his expression troubled. \"They report finding a manuscript matching our description of the Codex, dating back to their 1908 expedition to the South Pacific. According to their records, the book disappeared during a period of unexplained phenomena involving tidal anomalies and botanical mutations.\" He places the telegram on the desk. \"Most concerning is their observation that these events followed a thirty-year cycle. Prior to 1908, similar occurrences were documented in 1878, 1848, and so on back through their records.\"\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton's face pales. \"Thirty years... and always connected to the Codex. It's not a coincidence\u2014it's a pattern.\" He does some quick mental calculation. \"If the cycle holds, the next manifestation would be in 1967. We need to ensure these artifacts are secured well beyond that date, with clear warnings and protocols for those who come after us.\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): \"The Whispering Tide ebbs, but it always returns,\" Sebastian murmurs, almost to himself. \"What we experienced was merely one phase of something much larger and older than ourselves.\" He looks up at his colleagues. \"We've won a reprieve, not a victory. The knowledge in that book\u2014the entity it serves\u2014they operate on timescales beyond human comprehension.\"\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): Philip stands resolute. \"Then we must ensure that human comprehension evolves to meet this threat. I propose we form a small research group\u2014just the four of us, plus perhaps one or two trusted colleagues\u2014dedicated to understanding what we're facing.\" He looks at the vacuum chamber. \"That contains evidence, possibly even a fragment of the entity itself. Properly studied, under strict safety protocols, it might provide insights that could help future generations when the tide rises again.\"\n\nKEEPER: As the four of you discuss plans for containing both the artifacts and the knowledge of what transpired, the late afternoon sun casts long shadows across Dr. Armitage's office. Outside, students and faculty go about their normal routines, unaware of how close their world came to being subsumed by something beyond comprehension. For now, the Whispering Tide has receded, the doorway has closed, and reality has reasserted itself. But in the vacuum chamber, something pulses with faint bioluminescence, and occasionally, if one listens very carefully in the darkest hours of the night, one might hear what sounds like distant waves breaking on an alien shore\u2014a reminder that some barriers are not meant to be permanent, and that the tide always returns.\n\nPLAYER 1 (PHOENIX): Thornton looks out the window at the oblivious campus below. \"We should document everything while it's fresh in our minds. Future generations will need to know what signs to watch for.\" He straightens his tie, attempting to reclaim some normalcy. \"I never imagined my expertise in book conservation would lead to... this. But perhaps that's why we were able to stop it\u2014we each brought different knowledge to bear against something that defied conventional understanding.\"\n\nPLAYER 3 (SAGE): Sebastian nods solemnly. \"Knowledge preserved is our best defense. Though I wonder...\" He glances at the wrapped Codex. \"How many times has this cycle repeated? How many others before us thought they had ended the threat, only for it to return generations later?\" He sighs heavily. \"At least we know when to expect the next incursion. We can prepare\u2014or ensure our successors are prepared.\"\n\nPLAYER 2 (MORGAN): \"Science advances rapidly,\" Philip offers, a hint of optimism in his voice. \"Who knows what tools and understanding we'll have in thirty years? What seems incomprehensible now may be explicable then.\" He places a hand on the vacuum chamber. \"This is not just a containment vessel now\u2014it's our most valuable specimen, our window into understanding what waits beyond the veil.\" He looks at his colleagues with newfound resolve. \"We've faced the impossible and survived. That alone gives me hope.\"\n\nKEEPER: And with that, we'll bring our session to a close. The Whispering Tide has receded, but as you've all recognized, some threats operate on cycles beyond a single human lifetime. You've secured the artifacts and begun documenting your experiences, creating a foundation of knowledge for those who might face this entity in the future. Whether your preparations will be sufficient, whether the containment will hold, whether future scholars will heed your warnings\u2014these questions remain unanswered. But for now, Arkham continues its existence, unaware of how close it came to being consumed by something from beyond the stars, and unaware of the three scholars who now bear the burden of terrible knowledge and the responsibility it brings. Thank you all for playing, and remember\u2014in the world of Call of Cthulhu, victory is often temporary, but knowledge, for better or worse, endures."